        <item>
            <title><![CDATA[Stats::LikeR: high-performance basic statistics]]></title>
            <description><![CDATA['I got tired of writing CSV files just so I could run a t-test in R.<br>My data was nearly always in Perl already. I’d parse a log or a lab export, clean it up, dump it to disk, open R, run t.test, and paste the p-value back into the Perl script that built the report. Every one of those hops is a chance for a column to get reordered or an NA to turn into a zero without anyone noticing.']]></description>
            <pubDate>2026.10.28</pubDate>
            <guid isPermaLink="false">https://bioinformaticsdcondonwordpress.wordpress.com/2026/10/24/statistics-in-perl-statsliker/</guid>
            <link>https://bioinformaticsdcondonwordpress.wordpress.com/2026/10/24/statistics-in-perl-statsliker/</link>
            
            
            
            <author><![CDATA[david_condon]]></author>
            
                
            
            
        </item>
        
        <item>
            <title><![CDATA[Ex::Op: A drop-in replacement for Exporter]]></title>
            <description><![CDATA['Ex::Op is written in XS. It is a drop-in replacement for Exporter, and it gives your module an op keyword: declare a helper with op instead of sub, and calls to it skip the frame and @_ altogether.']]></description>
